# Scala Generics and Variance

## Overview

Scala's type system supports variance annotations for generic types, enabling flexible subtyping relationships.

## Type Parameters

```scala
// Simple generic class
class Container[A](value: A) {
  def get: A = value
}

// Multiple type parameters
class Pair[A, B](first: A, second: B)
```

## Variance Annotations

### Covariance (+A)

Producer position - can return subtypes:

```scala
trait Producer[+A] {
  def produce: A  // OK: A in return position
}

// If Dog <: Animal, then Producer[Dog] <: Producer[Animal]
class AnimalProducer extends Producer[Animal] {
  def produce: Animal = new Dog()
}
```

### Contravariance (-A)

Consumer position - can accept supertypes:

```scala
trait Consumer[-A] {
  def consume(a: A): Unit  // OK: A in parameter position
}

// If Dog <: Animal, then Consumer[Animal] <: Consumer[Dog]
```

### Invariance (A)

No subtyping relationship - exact type required:

```scala
trait Handler[A] {
  def get: A
  def set(a: A): Unit
}
```

## Type Bounds

```scala
// Upper bound: A must be subtype of Comparable[A]
class Sorter[A <: Comparable[A]]

// Lower bound: A must be supertype of B
def append[A >: B](list: List[A], elem: B): List[A]

// Multiple bounds
class Processor[A <: Serializable with Comparable[A]]
```

## Context Bounds

```scala
// Requires implicit Ordering[A] in scope
def sorted[A: Ordering](list: List[A]): List[A] =
  list.sorted
```

## Abstract Type Members

Alternative to type parameters:

```scala
trait Tokenizer {
  type Input
  def tokenize(input: Input): Token
}

class StringTokenizer extends Tokenizer {
  type Input = String
  def tokenize(input: String): Token = ???
}
```

## Union Types (Scala 2.13 workaround)

Scala 2.13 doesn't have native union types. Use sealed traits or Either:

```scala
// Option 1: Sealed trait hierarchy
sealed trait StringOrBytes
case class Str(value: String) extends StringOrBytes
case class Bytes(value: Array[Byte]) extends StringOrBytes

// Option 2: Either for binary unions
type StringOrInt = Either[String, Int]
```

